      "realLife": "When campus placement opens and 200 MCA students apply to the same company, every resume looks similar — same college, same degree, same semester. The cover letter is the only place where you sound like a person instead of a row in a spreadsheet.",
      "worked": "<strong>Sample letter of application (full block layout):</strong><br><br>Priya Sharma<br>Flat 12, Shivaji Nagar, Bhopal 462016<br>Phone: 98xxxxxx21 · Email: priya.sharma@example.com<br><br>28 September 2026<br><br>The HR Manager<br>Infotech Solutions Pvt. Ltd.<br>Plot 9, IT Park, Indore 452010<br><br>Dear Sir/Madam,<br><br>Subject: Application for the post of Junior Software Developer (Ref: ISPL/REC/2026/41)<br><br>With reference to your advertisement in <em>The Times of India</em> dated 22 September 2026, I wish to apply for the post of Junior Software Developer. I am currently pursuing my MCA at UIT-RGPV, Bhopal, and my coursework in programming and databases matches the requirements stated in your advertisement.<br><br>I have completed my BCA with 78% and am in the first year of MCA. I am comfortable with C, Java and SQL, and I have worked with HTML, CSS and JavaScript for front-end work. Your advertisement asks for a candidate with a strong foundation in programming logic and databases, which has been the main focus of my study so far.<br><br>During my BCA I built a Library Management System in Java with a MySQL database, which is now used by my college department to issue and track books. I also completed a six-week internship at Nexus Softwares, Bhopal, where I tested web modules and wrote defect reports. These gave me practical experience of working to a deadline within a team, and I am eager to apply that in a professional environment.<br><br>My detailed resume is enclosed for your kind consideration. I would welcome the opportunity of an interview at your convenience and can join immediately after completing my semester examinations in December 2026. Thank you for your time.<br><br>Yours faithfully,<br><br><em>(signature)</em><br>Priya Sharma<br><br>Encl.: Resume",
      "mistake": "Repeating the resume line by line in the cover letter. The resume lists WHAT you have done; the cover letter explains WHY it fits this particular job. If your letter could be sent to any company unchanged, it is not doing its job.",
      "examAnswer": "A letter of application, also known as a cover letter, is a formal letter written by a candidate to an employer while applying for a post, and it is always accompanied by a resume. Its purpose is to introduce the applicant, to relate the applicant's qualifications and skills specifically to the requirements of the advertised post, and to persuade the employer to grant an interview. It should be limited to one page and normally consists of an opening paragraph stating the post applied for and its source, a middle section presenting the applicant's qualifications, skills and relevant experience as evidence of suitability, and a closing paragraph requesting an interview and referring to the enclosed resume. An application may be solicited, written in response to an advertisement, or unsolicited, written when no vacancy has been advertised.",
      "remember": "Para 1 = which job. Para 2 = why me. Para 3 = proof. Para 4 = please call me.",
      "why": "This is literally the document that gets you your first job. Of everything in this syllabus, this is the page you will use soonest.",
      "practice": "Write a letter of application for a 'Software Trainee' post advertised on your college notice board, using the four-paragraph structure."
