        {
          "title": "Structure of a professional email",
          "text": "<strong>Subject line:</strong> specific and short — 'Request for leave on 5 October', not 'Hi' or 'Urgent!!'. <strong>Greeting:</strong> 'Dear Sir/Madam,' or 'Dear Ms. Nair,' — never 'Hey'. <strong>Body:</strong> two or three short paragraphs; purpose first, details second, request last. <strong>Sign-off:</strong> 'Regards,' or 'Yours sincerely,' followed by your full name, course and contact number."
        },
        {
          "title": "To, CC and BCC — used correctly",
          "text": "<strong>To</strong> is for the people who must <em>act</em> on the mail. <strong>CC</strong> (carbon copy) is for people who only need to <em>know</em>, and everyone can see them. <strong>BCC</strong> (blind carbon copy) hides the recipients from each other — use it when mailing a large list so you do not expose everybody's address. Never use BCC to secretly copy someone's boss into a dispute; if it is discovered, you lose all trust."
